    "title": "Network Nodes and Roles",
    "ref": "1.1.8",
    "q": "<p>Which of the following networking terms refers to the nodes that send and receive data traffic?</p><ul><li><strong>A)</strong> Network protocols</li><li><strong>B)</strong> Network links</li><li><strong>C)</strong> Intermediate nodes</li><li><strong>D)</strong> End systems</li></ul>",
    "ca": "<ul><li><strong>D) End systems</strong></li></ul>",
    "wc": "<ul><li><strong>Source and Destination Execution:</strong> End systems (also called hosts) sit at the edges of a network. They are the actual originators (sources) and ultimate consumers/destinations of data packets traversing the infrastructure.</li><li><strong>Service Consumption &amp; Provisioning:</strong> In modern client-server or peer-to-peer architectures, end systems run application-layer software (web browsers, email clients, database engines) to generate application payloads or deliver services.</li><li><strong>No Transit Forwarding:</strong> Unlike core networking hardware, an end system's primary role is not to forward third-party data traffic passing through the network, but to process data directed to or generated by its own network interface card (NIC).</li></ul>",
    "ml": "<ul><li><strong>OSI Layer Context:</strong> End systems operate primarily across all 7 layers of the Open Systems Interconnection (OSI) model, specifically executing upper-layer protocols (Layer 7 Application, Layer 6 Presentation, Layer 5 Session) down to Layer 1 Physical to transmit bits.</li><li><strong>Host Identity Parameters:</strong> An end system is identified on an IP network by a unique combination of a Layer 2 Media Access Control (MAC) address and a Layer 3 Internet Protocol (IP) address.</li><li><strong>Packet Encapsulation/Decapsulation Sequence:</strong> [ Application Data ] -&gt; (Encapsulation at Host A) -&gt; [ ETH Header | IP Header | TCP Header | Data ] | v (Transits Intermediate Nodes via Links) | [ ETH Header | IP Header | TCP Header | Data ] -&gt; (Decapsulation at Host B) -&gt; [ Application Data ]</li></ul>",
    "wa": "<ul><li><strong>A) Network protocols:</strong></li><ul><li><em>Why it is incorrect:</em> Protocols are intangible sets of software rules, standards, and algorithms (such as TCP/IP, HTTP, or ARP) that govern how data is formatted and transmitted. They are non-physical concepts, not device nodes.</li><li><em>What the question would need to ask instead:</em> \"Which networking term refers to the standardized sets of rules and formats that govern data communication between devices?\"</li></ul><li><strong>B) Network links:</strong></li><ul><li><em>Why it is incorrect:</em> Network links are the physical or logical transmission pathways (such as twisted-pair copper cables, fiber-optic strands, or radio frequency waves) that connect nodes together. They carry signals but do not create, process, or consume data traffic.</li><li><em>What the question would need to ask instead:</em> \"Which networking term describes the physical media or radio frequencies used to form communication pathways between network hardware?\"</li></ul><li><strong>C) Intermediate nodes:</strong></li><ul><li><em>Why it is incorrect:</em> Intermediate nodes (such as switches, routers, and firewalls) operate within the network fabric to forward, filter, and route data packets between endpoints. They inspect packet headers to direct traffic but are not the original senders or final destinations of user data payloads.</li><li><em>What the question would need to ask instead:</em> \"Which networking devices perform forwarding and routing functions to direct data packets across a network without serving as the ultimate destination for user traffic?\"</li></ul></ul>",
    "gl": "<ul><li><strong>End System (Host):</strong> Any end-user device (workstation, laptop, smartphone, server, IP camera) connected to a network that generates or receives user data.</li><li><strong>Intermediate Node:</strong> Networking hardware (switch, router, hub) responsible for relaying packets across the network path.</li><li><strong>Network Link:</strong> The physical cable or wireless channel connecting two network devices.</li><li><strong>Protocol:</strong> A shared language or set of technical rules that allows computers to communicate smoothly over a network.</li><li><strong>NIC (Network Interface Card):</strong> The hardware circuit board inside a host that connects it to the physical network media.</li></ul>",
    "rl": "<ul><li><strong>The Scenario:</strong> A medical clinic deploys a digital record network consisting of 20 doctor laptops, 2 central database servers, 3 Ethernet switches, and 1 core router.</li><li><strong>The Problem:</strong> The IT auditor must categorize all connected hardware assets into administrative categories to assign endpoint security software vs. network infrastructure maintenance.</li><li><strong>The Implementation:</strong> The laptops and database servers are classified as <strong>End Systems</strong> (hosts that process medical records). The switches and routers are classified as <strong>Intermediate Nodes</strong>.</li><li><strong>The Outcome:</strong> Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR) agents are installed strictly on the 22 End Systems, while firmware patches are pushed to the Intermediate Nodes, maintaining proper operational separation.</li></ul>",
    "mp": "<ul><li><strong>The Analogy:</strong> The Postal Service system.</li><li><strong>Mapping Incorrect Options:</strong></li><ul><li><em>Network protocols</em> are the addressing formatting guidelines (putting the ZIP code on the bottom right and the stamp on the top right).</li><li><em>Network links</em> are the asphalt highways and roads over which delivery trucks drive.</li><li><em>Intermediate nodes</em> are the regional mail sorting facilities and postal workers who pass envelopes along.</li></ul><li><strong>Mapping Correct Option:</strong></li><ul><li><em>End systems</em> are the sender writing the letter at home and the recipient opening the letter at their front door. They are the origin and ultimate destination of the message.</li></ul></ul>",
    "mn": "<ul><li><strong>\"End Systems = Ends of the Pipe\":</strong> Remember that <strong>End</strong> systems sit at the <strong>ends</strong> of the communication pipeline (Source and Destination).</li><li><strong>\"Intermediate In Between\":</strong> Intermediate nodes sit <strong>in between</strong> the endpoints to pass data along.</li></ul>",
    "ck": "<ul><li>[ ] Does the question ask for devices that <strong>send and receive</strong> (originate/consume) data? (Points directly toward <strong>End systems</strong>).</li><li>[ ] Is the prompt asking about forwarding hardware like routers or switches? (Points toward <strong>Intermediate nodes</strong>).</li><li>[ ] Is the question referring to cabling, copper, fiber, or wireless channels? (Points toward <strong>Network links</strong>).</li><li>[ ] Is the prompt asking about sets of rules, formatting, or standards? (Points toward <strong>Network protocols</strong>).</li><li>[ ] <strong>Remember:</strong> Hosts/End systems hold IP addresses and process application payloads; intermediate nodes just pass them along.</li></ul>",
