    "title": "Geographic Network Classifications (WAN vs. LAN)",
    "ref": "1.1.8",
    "q": "<p>A multinational corporation has its main office in New York and branch offices in London, Tokyo, and Sydney. The corporation needs a network that can connect all these offices together, allowing for seamless communication and data transfer. Which type of network would be MOST suitable for this setup?</p><ul><li><strong>A)</strong> Wide Area Network (WAN)</li><li><strong>B)</strong> Local Area Network (LAN)</li><li><strong>C)</strong> Small and Medium-sized Enterprise (SME) network</li><li><strong>D)</strong> Small Office/Home Office (SOHO) network</li></ul>",
    "ca": "<ul><li><strong>A) Wide Area Network (WAN)</strong></li></ul>",
    "wc": "<ul><li><strong>Global Geographic Span:</strong> A Wide Area Network (WAN) spans large physical distances, connecting local networks across cities, regions, countries, or continents.</li><li><strong>Telecommunications Provider Infrastructure:</strong> Unlike Local Area Networks (LANs) owned directly by the end organization, WAN connections rely on leased lines, fiber undersea cables, satellite links, and public telecommunication service providers (MSPs/ISPs).</li><li><strong>Interconnecting Distinct LANs:</strong> A corporate WAN acts as an umbrella network, tying together distant, independent Local Area Networks (New York LAN, London LAN, Tokyo LAN) into a single logical enterprise routing domain.</li></ul>",
    "ml": "<ul><li><strong>Geographic Network Classification Hierarchy:</strong></li></ul><pre>+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|  WAN (Wide Area Network): Global / Cross-Country (NY to London/Tokyo/Sydney)  |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n       |                                       |\n       v                                       v\n[ Metropolitan Area Network (MAN) ]      [ Campus Area Network (CAN) ]\n(Spans a single city, e.g., NYC)        (Spans university/corporate campus)\n       |                                       |\n       +-------------------+-------------------+\n                           |\n                           v\n          [ LAN (Local Area Network) ]\n          (Single building, office, or room)</pre><ul><li><strong>WAN Encapsulation &amp; Transport Technologies:</strong> WAN links utilize specialized Layer 2 framing protocols and transport technologies over provider networks, including MPLS (Multiprotocol Label Switching), SD-WAN (Software-Defined WAN), Broadband VPN tunnels, Carrier Ethernet, and DWDM fiber channels.</li></ul>",
    "wa": "<ul><li><strong>B) Local Area Network (LAN):</strong></li><ul><li><em>Why it is incorrect:</em> A LAN is strictly constrained to a single, localized geographic site (such as one floor, one office building, or a single room). Ethernet copper cabling and Wi-Fi cannot bridge oceans or span across countries without intermediate WAN infrastructure.</li><li><em>What the question would need to ask instead:</em> \"Which type of network is confined to a single physical building or office location, where all endpoints are connected using short-range local Ethernet cables and switches?\"</li></ul><li><strong>C) Small and Medium-sized Enterprise (SME) network:</strong></li><ul><li><em>Why it is incorrect:</em> An SME network describes the administrative scale and user capacity of a local organization (e.g., 50–200 users in a site). It does not represent a global inter-continental networking technology designed to span across New York, London, Tokyo, and Sydney.</li><li><em>What the question would need to ask instead:</em> \"What scale of network is designed to support 50 users in a single office space using structured cabling and managed switches?\"</li></ul><li><strong>D) Small Office/Home Office (SOHO) network:</strong></li><ul><li><em>Why it is incorrect:</em> A SOHO network is a tiny, local network meant for home offices or small shops (1–10 users). It lacks the routing capabilities, bandwidth, and telecommunication leased lines necessary to support a global multinational corporation.</li><li><em>What the question would need to ask instead:</em> \"Which network type is designed for a single residential workspace housing a handful of local devices behind a consumer internet router?\"</li></ul></ul>",
    "gl": "<ul><li><strong>WAN (Wide Area Network):</strong> A network spanning long geographical distances (cities, countries) using telecom provider links.</li><li><strong>LAN (Local Area Network):</strong> A high-speed network limited to a small physical area like a single building or room.</li><li><strong>MAN (Metropolitan Area Network):</strong> A network covering a single town or city.</li><li><strong>MPLS (Multiprotocol Label Switching):</strong> A high-performance telecom mechanism used to direct data from one network node to another along private WAN paths.</li><li><strong>ISP (Internet Service Provider):</strong> A commercial telecommunications company that provides access to the global Internet and WAN circuits.</li></ul>",
    "rl": "<ul><li><strong>The Scenario:</strong> Global Trading Corp operates financial trading desks in New York, London, and Tokyo.</li><li><strong>The Problem:</strong> Traders in Tokyo need to access central database servers physically located in the New York headquarters with encrypted, guaranteed low-latency connectivity.</li><li><strong>The Implementation:</strong> The company contracts with AT&amp;T and BT to establish a private <strong>Wide Area Network (WAN)</strong> using MPLS circuits and SD-WAN edge routers in each branch.</li><li><strong>The Outcome:</strong> Employees in London, Tokyo, and Sydney log into their computers and seamlessly access the New York data vault as if the servers were sitting in the room next door.</li></ul>",
    "mp": "<ul><li><strong>The Analogy:</strong> Mail delivery systems.</li><li><strong>Mapping Incorrect Options:</strong></li><ul><li><em>LAN</em> is an inter-office mail cart inside a single skyscraper floor. Fast and efficient for handing a memo to the desk next to you, but the cart cannot roll down the highway to another country.</li><li><em>SOHO network</em> is a personal whiteboard hanging in your kitchen.</li><li><em>SME network</em> is the internal mailroom of a local 3-story office building.</li></ul><li><strong>Mapping Correct Option:</strong></li><ul><li><em>WAN</em> is the international cargo shipping network (cargo ships, cargo jets, customs checkpoints). Specifically built to carry packages across thousands of miles of oceans and international borders to connect far-away cities.</li></ul></ul>",
    "mn": "<ul><li><strong>\"WAN = World Wide / Wide Area\":</strong> If it crosses city, state, or national borders (e.g., NY to Tokyo), it is always a <strong>WAN</strong>.</li><li><strong>\"LAN = Local / Limited\":</strong> LANs stay local to one roof. WANs cross the globe.</li></ul>",
    "ck": "<ul><li>[ ] Does the scenario list multiple distant cities, states, or countries (e.g., NY, London, Tokyo, Sydney)? (Points directly toward <strong>Wide Area Network [WAN]</strong>).</li><li>[ ] Is the network described as spanning long distances across public or telecom leased lines? (Points directly toward <strong>WAN</strong>).</li><li>[ ] Is the network strictly inside a single building or room? (Points toward <strong>LAN</strong>).</li><li>[ ] <strong>Remember:</strong> Organizations own their LAN hardware directly; organizations lease WAN connections from telecommunications providers.</li></ul>",
